| Table | How | Evidence |
|-------|-----|----------|
| **`memo`** | Explicit `DELETE` | Final step in `Store.DeleteMemo` → `driver.DeleteMemo`. SQL: `DELETE FROM memo WHERE id = …` in `store/db/postgres/memo.go:244-246` (same in sqlite:237-239, mysql:259-261). Service deletes comment memos first, then the target memo (`memo_service.go:567-581`). |
| **`memo_relation`** | Explicit cleanup (2 passes) | `Store.DeleteMemo` calls `DeleteMemoRelation` twice — by `memo_id`, then by `related_memo_id` (`store/memo.go:141-147`). SQL: `DELETE FROM memo_relation WHERE …` in `store/db/postgres/memo_relation.go:153-164` (sqlite:137-150, mysql:126-137). |
| **`attachment`** | Explicit cleanup loop | `Store.DeleteMemo` lists attachments for the memo, then calls `DeleteAttachment` for each (`store/memo.go:148-157`). `DeleteAttachment` → `driver.DeleteAttachment` (`store/attachment.go:132-148`). SQL: `DELETE FROM attachment WHERE id = …` in `store/db/postgres/attachment.go:211-212` (sqlite:211-212, mysql:230-231). |
| **`memo_share`** | DB `ON DELETE CASCADE` | No application delete call. Schema FK: `FOREIGN KEY (memo_id) REFERENCES memo(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` in `store/migration/sqlite/LATEST.sql:101-109` (postgres:100-108, mysql:100-108). Migration comment: `store/migration/sqlite/0.27/04__memo_share.sql:3`. Rows removed when the `memo` row is deleted. |

---

